The warning means you tried to install a package called crypto from npm, but Node.js has its own powerful built-in crypto engine that requires zero installation. [1, 2, 3] 
The Error Code 13 usually means the installation failed due to an operating system permission conflict or a configuration crash caused by attempting to overwrite core modules.
Here is exactly how to fix your script and resolve the npm environment crash.
------------------------------
## 🛠️ Step 1: Remove the Broken Dependency Reference
Since crypto is native to Node.js, you must remove the explicit dependency reference from your package.json manifest file to stop npm from crashing. Run this in your terminal: [4] 

npm uninstall crypto

------------------------------
## 💻 Step 2: Clean the Script Imports
Open your xss_fsm_crawler.js and xss_fsm_crawler2.js files. Look at the top line where dependencies are requested.
If you are using modern JavaScript ES Modules (import), update your import statements to target Node's native module paths: [5] 

// Change this line:import crypto from 'crypto';
// To this (explicitly targets Node's built-in namespace):import crypto from 'node:crypto';

If your scripts are written using CommonJS (require), update the declaration line like this:

// Change this line:const crypto = require('crypto');
// To this:const crypto = require('node:crypto');

------------------------------
## ⚡ Step 3: Clear and Reinstall Clean Dependencies
To resolve the lingering Error Code 13 workspace lock, wipe your temporary cache registries and re-run your clean Puppeteer engine setup:

rm -rf node_modules package-lock.json
npm cache clean --force
npm install puppeteer

This ensures your directory tree stays clean and your automated FSM graph scanner executes without environment conflicts.
